\section{What the Scan Orders}

Poletto and Sarkar's \emph{Linear Scan Register Allocation} orders
intervals by their starts and keeps a set of active allocations.
Number machine program points, allow separate input and output
positions when an instruction reads before writing, and use
half-open intervals $[s,e)$. When a new interval begins at $s$, expire
any active interval with end at most $s$. With a free register, assign
it; otherwise compare the new interval with the active one that ends
furthest away. The basic policy spills the later-ending candidate:
it prefers to keep registers assigned to values whose intervals will
release them sooner. End positions are not next-use positions, so the
policy can evict a value that is about to be read.

With two registers, let $a=[0,5)$, $b=[1,9)$, $c=[3,6)$,
and $d=[5,7)$. The scan initially gives the registers to $a$ and $b$.
On encountering $c$ it chooses $b$ as the spill candidate and
gives $c$ the register formerly assigned to $b$. In basic unsplit
scan, that changes $b$'s whole-interval assignment; it does not by
itself promise that the register copy of $b$ survives until point 3.
At point 5, $a$ has expired and $d$ gets the first register. Forgetting
to expire $a$ at equality would introduce an unnecessary spill.
Without spills, for contiguous intervals and interchangeable registers,
ordering by start succeeds whenever at most $K$ intervals cover any
point: all unexpired earlier intervals cover the current start, so an
exhausted register set would witness $K+1$ overlaps.

\section{Holes and Deliberate Splits}

