An interval can enclose blocks in the linear layout where the value
is not live. Wimmer and Franz's \emph{Linear Scan Register Allocation
on SSA Form} uses lifetime holes, split intervals, and an edge
resolution phase. At a point inside a hole, a register assignment
can be inactive rather than permanently expired. When considering
that register for another interval, compute the first future
intersection with any inactive owner. Taking the register now is
legal only up to that position unless one of the assignments changes.

For one register, lay out entry, two alternative blocks, and a
join in that order. $A$ has live pieces $[1,4)$ in entry and
$[10,11)$ in the second arm; it is not used in the first arm
$[4,10)$. A live-in $B$ has a valid stack home and is needed at
8 in the first arm and at 12 in the join. Allocate $B$ to the
register for the first arm, where $A$ is inactive, and keep $B$
in memory in the second arm, where $A$ retains the register.
Both arms meet at the join, which expects $B$ in a register.
The first arm can keep its copy if no instruction clobbers it;
the second arm must reload after the last use of $A$. In particular
the first arm does not execute a spill simply because position 10
follows it in layout. Cooper and Simpson's \emph{Live Range Splitting
in a Graph Coloring Register Allocator} shows that the same choice
to localize spilling can also be made in a coloring framework; it is
not intrinsically part of the scan.

\section{Resolving Edges and Phi Inputs}

In SSA (Chapter~\ref{ch:ssa}), a phi input is a use on its incoming
edge, not at the start of the join block. Assign locations to the
predecessor's phi input and the successor's corresponding result.
Resolve these and other split-range mappings together as one
parallel copy on that edge. For example, one incoming edge needs
$r_1\gets r_2$ and $r_2\gets r_1$. Sequential evaluation would
duplicate one of the values. With scratch $s$, the sequence
$s\gets r_1$, $r_1\gets r_2$, $r_2\gets s$ preserves both; a
private stack temporary can supply scratch when a register is
unavailable and legal moves allow it. Use an edge block on a
critical edge, where neither endpoint can execute the shuffles
unconditionally without affecting a different path.

A useful test has two predecessors with opposite input locations
and makes both merged values observable. Execute each predecessor
with different symbolic inputs and check that both paths establish
the same successor location contract. A scan that assigns
nonoverlapping ranges but omits this last step can nevertheless
compute the wrong result. The validator of
Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-240} reasons about these boundary facts directly.

