\section{Pick a Legal Split before the First Collision}

A lifetime hole gives a register a temporary opening, not necessarily
enough room for the next useful part of a range. Suppose the allocator
has one register and a fixed owner whose live pieces are already known.
A second value has a current stack copy throughout its lifetime. To
allocate one continuous register piece for the second value, find the
first collision with the owner, then choose a split boundary no later
than the collision. Assume cuts are ordered instruction subpositions
where a move is legal, and that the entry to the piece permits a
reload. Reads at a cut belong to the suffix in this convention.

\begin{sudocode}[Allocate a prefix against a fixed owner]
\State $L\gets$ first intersection with owner's live pieces
\If{there is no such intersection}
  \State $L\gets$ end of request
\EndIf
\State $c\gets$ last legal cut in $(start,L]$
  \Comment{Never round forward}
\If{no cut exists or no read lies in $[start,c)$}
  \State \Return no register prefix
\EndIf
\State reload at $start$; assign register through $[start,c)$
\State enqueue suffix from $c$ if nonempty
  \Comment{Its stack copy is still current}
\end{sudocode}

Let the request start at 5 and end at 15, with reads at 7 and 13.
The owner holds the register at $[10,12)$. Legal cuts after entry are
8, 11, 12, and 15. The first collision is at 10, so split at 8,
not 11. The value 42 reloads for the read at 7. At 10 the owner
can use its own value 99; a later scan decision can reload 42 at
12 for the read at 13. Compare the observed values $42,99,42$ with
a baseline that always reloads on demand. If the first read moves
from 7 to 8, there is no useful prefix by this policy; allocating one
would insert a load without serving a read.

The half-open boundary invariant prevents simultaneous ownership.
Choosing the latest legal cut is a heuristic, not a requirement:
shortening the prefix to just past its last read can free a register
for other ranges even earlier. A dirty suffix would need a different
plan; the clean stack copy is what makes this split store-free.
